Dart Data Types and Variables
|============================|

Data Types
----------
Data types define the kind of value a variable can store.

Data Type             Description

1.  Int               Whole numbers (e.g, 25, 30)
2.  double            Decimal numbers (e.g, 99.5, 90.4)
3.  num               Integers or decimal numbers (e.g, 25, 99.5)
4.  String            Text (e.g, "flutter")
5.  bool              Boolean values (true or false)
6.  List              Ordered collection of items, duplicates allowed (e.g, List<int> number = [1,2,3,4,1])
7.  Set               Collection of Unique items (e.g, Set<int> number = {1,2,3,4})
8.  Map               Collection of Key-Value pairs (e.g, Map<String, int> scores = {'John' : 90})
10. dynamic           Allows values of different types (e.g, 10, 99.5, "flutter")
                      (Note: Prefer specific types such as int, String, and List<String> whenever possible. Use dynamic only when necessary.)


Mutable vs Immutable
|===================|

1. Mutable 
----------
An Object is mutable when its contents or state can be changed after creation. 

final numbers = [1,2,3];
numbers.add(4);
print(numbers); //[1,2,3,4]

2. Immutable 
------------
An object is immutable when its state cannot be changed after creation. 

const numbers = [1,2,3];
//numbers.add(4); //Error


Important = final prevents reassignment of variable, but it does not automatically make the object immutable. 

final name = "flutter";
//name = "dart"; //Error

final items = [1,2,3];
items.add(4); //Allowed

Variable Declaration Keywords
|============================|

Dart Provides 4 commonly used variable declaration options: var, dynamic, final and const. 

1. var - Type Inference
var allows Dart to infer a variable's type for its initail value. Once inferred, the variable's type remain fixed

var name = "flutter"; //String type

name = "dart" //String only allowed
//name = 100; // Error

2. dynamic - Flexible Type
dynamic allows a variable to hold values of different types. Type errors may therefore appear at runtime.

dynamic value = 10;
value = "flutter";
value = true;

3. final - Assign Once
final allows a variable to be assigned only once. Its value can be determined at runtime.

final currentTime = DateTime.now();
// currentTime = DateTime.now(); //Error

4. const - Compile-time Constant
const is used for values that must known at compile time. A constant value cannot be reassigned or mutated.

const pi = 3.14;
const appName = "Flutter App";

// pi = 3.14159; //Error

var vs dynamic
--------------
var                                                           dynamic

- Dart infers the type from the initial value                - The variable can hold different types.
- The inferred type remains fixed.                           - The type can change during execution. 
- provides stronger compile-time type checking               - Provides less compile-time type safety. 


fianl vs const
--------------
final                                                         const

- Assigned only once                                          - Compile-time constant. 
- Can hold a runtime value.                                   - Value must be know at compile time. 
- Prevents variable reassignment.                             - Prevents reassignment and constant-object mutation. 


_____________________________________________________

1. var: Infer the type automatically.
2. dynamic: Allows values of different type.
3. final: Assign a variable only once. 
4. const: Define a compile-time constant.
5. Mutable: Define a complie-time constant. 
6. Immutable: The object's state cannot change.
_____________________________________________________